now joining us in the studio is the  former Education Secretary Michael Gove  hello thank you for being with us now  owning before we start I'm keen to get  your thoughts as someone who's still  someone very well connected with lots of  people in the cabinet Jeremy Hunt health  secretary hasn't tweeted for five days  he hasn't been seen in front of a camera  since those NHS cyberattacks do you  think it's okay yes I absolutely I'm not  Jeremy was actually in Cobra yesterday  helping to coordinate the response to  the cyber attacks as health secretary he  has that managerial responsibility and  last week I saw Jeremy on the Robert  Peston program we appeared together and  he was unveiling a very exciting and  important mental health initiative  Jeremy's been and will be an absolutely  superb health secretary and I think that  he's a real asset to this country into  the government so why hasn't he been  anywhere since this huge very worrying  cyber attack because he's a hands-on  health secretary and it will be his  responsibility when he's discharging  very well to deal with the situation  that arose but because it's a crime was  also appropriate that amber rod  explained what the steps are as Home  Secretary responsible for dealing with  crime in order to try to make sure that  the people responsible for this attack  are actually tracked down and dealt with  fair enough hands off health secretary  perhaps hands and all hands off slightly  when it comes to the media hands on when  it comes to dealing with the attacks  that putting management of the health  service first is the single most  important thing that any health  secretary would do I know from my time  in government that sometimes there would  be a request from the media that you  might want to accept in order to appear  but your first priority was to get  things right in your own department to  talk to those people who were  responsible for delivering services and  to make sure that patients or in my case  parents and pupils came first now the  big policy announcement from the  Conservative Party today is this pledge  to build more council homes is this  another example of Theresa May stealing  from the Ed Miliband paper no I don't  think so we had Alex Salmond on earlier  speaking from his increasingly marginal  seat in Scotland talking about what he  termed a hard right Tory government the  truth as your question implies is very  different Teresa may and her team occupy  the mainstream middle ground of British  politics and one of the difficulties  that we've had in the past is that  labour in power  didn't build the number of homes  including the number of homes for rent  that we needed and Teresa is intervening  in order to ensure that ordinary working  people have the chance not only to have  a home to rent but in due course our  home to buy as well now you say that  Teresa most government is all about the  kind of middle ground  some people would dispute that when it  comes to brexit which of course is you  know the big one of the big issues of  this election to Reason a promising to  take the UK out of the EU after the  single market you must be delighted as  someone who was in that vote leave  campaign because she seems to swallowed  your playbook well I am very pleased  that Teresa may is taking Britain out of  the European Union and I think whether  people voted to leave or to remain and  of course I respect the strong and  idealistic sentiment but motivated many  people to remain the question in this  election is who do you want across that  table from jean-claude Juncker do you  want to resume providing strong and  stable leadership or do you want Jeremy  Corbyn we've already seen Jeremy Corbyn  during the course of this general  election campaign short out a series of  nonsensical policies he would be a  disaster as Britain's representative in  those talks so whatever you want from  brexit I personally do want us outside  the single market and outside the  customs union whatever you want juries  amaze the person best equipped to  deliver it because I wonder if you ever  have a moment of doubt or concern about  that because the war words between  number 10 and Brussels does seem to be  intensifying to resume effectively  accusing Brussels of meddling in the  election and this is what the foreign  secretary Boris Johnson said yesterday  they're going to play dirty they're  going to try and bleed this country  white with their bill do you agree with  Boris Johnson that the ear we're going  to try and bleed this country white I  think it is the case that the EU will  put have put quite a big price tag on  our departure that's part of negotiation  and if you want the best outcome for it  and then you need to have a tough  negotiator teresa has been described in  the past  indeed on Sky News by Ken Clark as a  bloody difficult woman and Teresa has  actually turned that phrase round and  said what you need is someone who will  be difficult and determined on Britain's  behalf in those negotiations and that's  why I think at this election we can't  afford to take the risk of having anyone  else other than Teresa mayor's our prime  do you accept  we will have to pay some money because  Boss Johnson seemed to apply that is the  EU you should be paying us to leave  there's absolutely no need for us in law  to pay money as we leave now the final  details of this negotiation will be up  for the prime minister and for the  cabinet to agree but in law the House of  Lords have spelt out in an authoritative  report that there is no need for us to  pay a penny when we leave and whatever  happens once we've left it will be clear  that we will be able to take back  control of the money that we currently  give to the European Union hundreds of  millions of pounds a week now we've been  talking about Boris Johnson there I  think it's fair to say a few eyebrows  were raised in Europe when he was made  foreign secretary  last year you famously described Boris  Johnson saying he cannot provide the  leadership for the task ahead you  comfortable with him playing a role in  the negotiations yes I am last year of  course during the the tumult of  leadership election I put myself forward  and we ran operate very well and  therefore by implication I was saying  that I thought that I was better  equipped than Teresa may I've now had a  chance over the course of the last 11  months or so to look at how Boris and  Teresa have performed in their  respective roles I think Teresa has got  every major decision right as Prime  Minister and I think Boris has been an  asset to the country and to the  government as foreign secretary I think  on facing down Russia over its behavior  in Syria in making sure that we forged  strong relationships with the new  American administration and also in  ensuring that international development  money and Britain's soft power is  playing a part in helping to reconstruct  Africa and those nations which have been  broken by external events he has shown a  strong leadership role and it's also the  case that having someone who was a  hugely popular champion of the vote  leave movement there at the heart of  government well I think that's something  that is all to the good David Cameron  someone else who we've seen out and  about on the campaign trail this week  let's have a look what he said about not  very coded message this perhaps for  tourism oh it's so important that the  Conservatives win and win well so  Theresa can stand up to people who want  an extreme brexit and he said  here and elsewhere so he's talked about  people like you isn't he people who want  a hard brexit out of the single market  key brexit ears does treasom a need a  big majority so she can stand up to some  of those pesky brexit ears i think she  she deserves his strong majority so that  her hand can be strengthened in dealing  with brussels but speaking for myself i  don't know whether or not i'm oh are  hard 